dax: finish a write's copy from kernel memory

A filesystem whose copy-on-write gives a write new blocks before the copy,
keeping nothing of what whole pages held, as Fxfs's DAX does, has the
write's bytes in kernel memory first, so that the copy can't stop short.
A task killed between its iomap and its copy stopped it all the same,
which left the new blocks' zeros where the file's data was. Such a copy
waits for nothing and is bounded: it isn't stopped.

diff --git a/fs/dax.c b/fs/dax.c
--- a/fs/dax.c
+++ b/fs/dax.c
@@ -1693,7 +1693,11 @@ static int dax_iomap_iter(struct iomap_iter *iomi, struct iov_iter *iter)
 		bool recovery = false;
 		void *kaddr;
 
-		if (fatal_signal_pending(current)) {
+		/*
+		 * A copy from kernel memory waits for nothing, and may be what a
+		 * filesystem's copy-on-write gave up the old blocks for.
+		 */
+		if (!iov_iter_is_kvec(iter) && fatal_signal_pending(current)) {
 			ret = -EINTR;
 			break;
 		}
